# Memory fault fuzz (black-parrot#312, #313): 100 generated cases.
# GENERATED by src/gen_mem_fault_fuzz.py --seed 1 --count 100 -- do not edit.
#
# Every case does one load, store or instruction fetch, either translated
# through a static Sv39 table (data: M-mode with MPRV=1, MPP=S or U; fetch: a
# real S/U-mode mret to the target) or untranslated in M-mode.  Valid and
# faulting targets are mixed.  The M-mode trap handler compares mcause, mtval
# and mepc (the faulting instruction) with the values the generator's
# reference model computed, then resumes at the next case.  A load that does
# not trap must return the marker word.  A fetch that does not fault
# executes an `ecall`, so every fetch case ends in the handler.
#
# Layout: the tables live in .pt (misaligned_fault.ld, 0x80010000) as static data;
#   0x80010000 pt_root
#   0x80011000 pt_a1
#   0x80012000 pt_a0
#   0x80013000 pt_b1
#   0x80014000 pt_c1
#   0x80015000 pt_c0
#   0x80016000 data_page
# Leaves map the marker page (data) or the `ecall` at 0x80000004 (fetch), at 4K,
# 2M and 1G level; superpage leaves use PA 0x80000000 and select the target
# through the VA offset.  Every translated case uses its own VA page, or
# reuses one earlier case's page to exercise TLB hits.
#
# Result: store 0 to the host finish register -> PASS, otherwise the 1-based
# case number (255: setup check) is the low byte.  A failure also prints
# seven 64-bit hex words on the host putint port: case, expected cause,
# got cause (-1: no trap, -2: load data mismatch), expected mtval, got mtval,
# expected mepc, got mepc.
# The comment above each case tells the parameters and expected result.
#
# Privileged-spec rules checked: Sv39 walk (canonical VA, V/R/W/X/U, reserved
# bits 60:54, non-leaf A/D/U, misaligned superpage, SUM, MXR), mcause, mtval
# = faulting VA, mepc.  mtval is not compared when the address does not fit
# in 39 bits sign extended: the spec lets an implementation turn such an
# address into another invalid address.  The trap cause is still checked.
#
# BlackParrot platform behaviour checked (not spec rules): (a) A=0, or D=0
# on a store, raises a page fault, the core does not update A/D (Svade
# style); (b) a fetch from a high-I/O domain is an access fault; (c) any
# access at or above 2^40, and any PTE whose PPN does not fit a 40-bit
# physical address, is an access fault.
#
# Excluded: loads/stores to a high-I/O domain not enabled in hio_mask.
# platform_guide.md says they fault, but the RTL does not today
# (bp_mmu.sv applies hio_fault_v to the instruction access fault only).
# Generate them with --io-data.

    .section .text.init, "ax"
    .globl _start
_start:
    j       main
tramp:                           # PA 0x80000004, fetch target
    ecall

    .align 2
trap_handler:
    li      t0, 0x20000
    csrc    mstatus, t0         # MPRV off
    csrr    s2, mcause
    csrr    s3, mtval
    csrr    s1, mepc
    bne     s2, s9, fail
    xor     t0, s3, s10
    and     t0, t0, s4          # s4 = 0: mtval not compared (see header)
    bnez    t0, fail
    bne     s1, s8, fail        # mepc
    li      t0, 0x1800
    csrs    mstatus, t0         # MPP = M
    csrw    mepc, s11
    mret

